# Edge Locations

CloudFront uses a worldwide network of **Edge Locations**.

An Edge Location is a location where CloudFront can cache and serve content closer to users.

For example:

```text
                    CloudFront
                        │
       ┌────────────────┼────────────────┐
       ↓                ↓                ↓
   Edge Location    Edge Location    Edge Location
      India            Europe             USA
       │                │                │
       ↓                ↓                ↓
     Users            Users             Users
```

The goal is to serve content from a location that is closer to the user.

---

# Origin

The **origin** is the original location from which CloudFront retrieves content when it isn't already available in the cache.

Common origins include:

```text
S3 Bucket
EC2
Application Load Balancer
API Gateway
Custom HTTP Server
```

For example:

```text
User
 ↓
CloudFront
 ↓
S3 Bucket
```

Here:

- CloudFront = CDN
- S3 = Origin
- User = Client

---

# CloudFront Cache

One of the main features of CloudFront is caching.

Suppose a website has:

```text
logo.png
style.css
app.js
image.jpg
```

A user requests:

```text
example.com/logo.png
```

If the file is already cached at the relevant CloudFront edge location:

```text
User
 ↓
CloudFront
 ↓
Cache Hit
 ↓
logo.png
```

The origin doesn't need to be contacted.

If it isn't cached:

```text
User
 ↓
CloudFront
 ↓
Cache Miss
 ↓
Origin
 ↓
CloudFront
 ↓
Cache the content
 ↓
User
```

---

# Cache Hit vs Cache Miss

## Cache Hit

A requested object is available in the CloudFront cache.

```text
User
 ↓
CloudFront
 ↓
Cache Hit
 ↓
Content
```

This is faster because CloudFront doesn't need to retrieve the object from the origin.

## Cache Miss

The requested object isn't currently available in the cache.

```text
User
 ↓
CloudFront
 ↓
Cache Miss
 ↓
Origin
 ↓
Content
 ↓
CloudFront Cache
 ↓
User
```

CloudFront can then cache the response according to the configured caching behavior.

---

# TTL — Time To Live

CloudFront uses **TTL (Time To Live)** to determine how long an object can remain cached before it needs to be checked/retrieved again according to the configured cache behavior and origin headers.

For example:

```text
TTL = 1 hour
```

An object can remain cached for the configured period.

After the relevant cache lifetime expires, CloudFront may need to obtain a fresh version from the origin.

TTL is important because it affects the balance between:

```text
Long TTL
→ Better caching
→ Fewer origin requests

Short TTL
→ Fresher content
→ More origin requests
```

